SUMMARY:Rotary Club of Davis Centennial Celebration
DESCRIPTION:Rotary Club of Davis to celebrate 100 years of service\nThe Rotary Club of Davis will mark its centennial with a community celebration from 5 to 8\np.m. Sunday\, Oct. 4\, at the Davis Veterans Memorial Center.\nFounded in 1926 under the auspices of Rotary International\, the Rotary Club of Davis has spent\na century combining service\, fellowship and civic involvement. The original Davis club is now\none of three Rotary clubs in the city\, along with Sunrise Rotary Club of Davis and Sunset Rotary\nClub of Davis.\nTickets for the centennial dinner are $45 and include dinner by Simply Satisfied Catering. For\ntickets\, contact Donna Prebys at 630-803-7954.\nDave Scheiber will serve as master of ceremonies. The evening’s program will include a\npresentation on a century of Davis Rotary by Michael Peterson and Jonathan Bayless\, a Rotary\ntrivia contest led by Bruce and Marjorie Hartsough\, and remarks about the club’s future from\nPrebys\, the club’s 2026-27 president.\nThe club began with 25 charter members and\, by the early 1930s\, had grown to nearly 30\nmembers. Rotarians met Mondays at noon at the Terminal Cafe at Second and G streets. Early\nactivities included support for the Boy Scout Cabin\, downtown Christmas-tree decorating\,\ncontributions to a University Farm student-loan fund\, sponsorship of a Picnic Day float trophy\,\nand individual assistance through the club’s charity committee.\nAs Davis and UC Davis expanded during the 1960s\, the club developed a more formal approach\nto community projects. Its longtime luncheon meetings were held at the Fellowship Hall of\nDavis Community Church through the early 1990s.\nThe 1970s brought several important changes. The club launched its Turkey Barbecue that spring\nas a major fundraiser for local projects. Now known as the Rotary Barbecue\, the annual Central\nPark event continues with Sunrise Rotary as co-sponsor.\nService projects from that era included planting trees and improving landscaping at Davis High\nSchool\, where Rotarians created Rotary Grove\, and landscaping Davis Community Park with 75\ntrees along a walkway leading to the pool. The club also began international service in Fiji\, first\nhelping cover freight costs for educational supplies and later funding four years of high-school\ntuition for a student there.\nBy the 1980s\, membership grew from about 110 members to 138. The club supported repairs to\nthe Boy Scout Cabin\, built a parking lot at International House and a snack bar at the Little\nLeague field on F Street\, and continued its Fiji educational\, medical and housing-support efforts.\nThe club also joined Rotary International’s PolioPlus initiative after it began in 1986.\nThrough changing times\, the Rotary Club of Davis has remained focused on service projects\,\ncommunity partnerships and fellowship among members. More information is available at\nrotaryclubofdavis.com. \n 
